<p class="status"><b>Private page.</b> It is not listed on Google and is not in the website menu. It will be removed before the website opens to the public.</p>

<h2 id="how">How to review</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Open an article</strong> from the list below and read it on your phone or computer.</li>
<li><strong>Write your changes on paper.</strong> Use the <a href="/review/sheets/">printable review sheets</a> or any plain paper. Write the article number and the heading of each section you want changed.</li>
<li><strong>Take a clear photo of each page</strong> and send the photos to the site owner. The changes are made on the website, and you can read the article again here.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="articles">The 6 draft articles</h2>
<p>Each one shows a “Draft preview” banner at the top. Nothing here is published yet.</p>
<ol>
{% for a in review.articles %}<li><strong>Article {{ a.n }}.</strong> <a href="{{ a.url }}">{{ a.title }}</a><br>{{ a.what }}</li>
{% endfor %}</ol>

<h2 id="look-for">What to look for</h2>
<ul>
<li>Is anything medically wrong, out of date or misleading?</li>
<li>Is anything missing that a doctor would expect to see?</li>
<li>Is it clear for a busy doctor, or too basic, or too technical?</li>
<li>Does any sentence claim more than we can prove? Every article should say “researched, not yet tested” and show no score.</li>
<li>Is it fair about risks, patient consent and who is responsible for the final decision?</li>
<li>Your own note: two or three sentences, in your own words, on one point most reviews miss. Nobody else writes this.</li>
</ul>

